7. The learned Magistrate, by order dt.18-03-2021, without issuing notice to Respondent No.2 and without undertaking any prima-facie adjudication on the allegations against her, held that:
i) The prayer restraining alienation of property is "civil in nature";
ii) There was no shared household between the appellant and Respondent No.2;
iii) Hence the petition was not maintainable against Respondent No.2;
iv) Consequently, Respondent No.2 was directed to be deleted from the array of respondents.
8. Aggrieved by the impugned order, the present appeal is preferred on the following:-
Grounds of Appeal a) The impugned order dated 18-3-2021 passed by the trial court is contrary to law and facts of the case.

My findings to the above points as follows:
Point No. 1 :- In the Negative Point No. 2 :- As per final order for the following;
REASONS 24. Point No.1:-

The Trial Court is hereby strictly directed to:
 (a) Issue notice to both respondents forthwith;
 (b) Re-adjudicate I.A. No.1 afresh strictly under Sections 18, 19, 20 and 23 of the Act;
 (c) Specifically consider interim protection against alienation of the schedule property, Return of Stridhana, Monetary relief including rent, security deposit and litigation expenses, Medical and educational expenses of the hearing- impaired minor child.
Rajalakshmi J vs K Lokesha on 19 January, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/190454000/
